Native Linux x86_64 bootstrap installation

The toolchain selection contains GCC/G++ 13.5.0, binutils 2.41, musl 1.2.5 and static C/C++ runtimes.
The userland selection contains Bash 5.2.15, Make 4.2.1, coreutils 6.10, findutils 4.2.33, diffutils 2.7,
sed 4.0.9, grep 2.4, gawk 3.0.4, tar 1.12, gzip 1.2.4, bzip2 1.0.8,
patch 2.5.9, m4 1.4.7, Flex 2.6.4 and Bison 3.4.1.

The all selection combines both.

Keep the installation directory together when copying or moving it. Add its
bin directory to PATH. The launchers locate their tools, headers, libraries
and generator data relative to the installation using Linux /proc/self/exe.
No absolute workspace path is compiled into them.

    gcc hello.c -o hello
    g++ -std=gnu++20 -pthread hello.cc -o hello
    make -j4

Compilation and linking are static by default. The selected target is native
x86_64 only; shared executables, multilib and other GCC languages are omitted.
The C++ language/library support is that of GCC 13.5.0, whose default is
C++17; C++20 facilities such as std::format, ranges and the time zone
database, with the time zone data built into libstdc++, are available. Explicit -nostdinc and
-nostdinc++ suppress the corresponding installed default include directories.

The compiler ignores inherited host compiler/include/library search variables;
use explicit -I, -L and other compiler arguments for project dependencies.
Launchers prepend this installation's bin to PATH and select the installed
Bash. Bison, Flex, gawk, locate and updatedb also get the installed m4, Bison
data, awk library and locate database. Other programs keep the caller's values
of those settings. An unset TMPDIR defaults to /tmp. An explicitly set
TMPDIR is preserved. Makefile and command-line SHELL settings keep their
normal precedence over the installed default shell.

The locate database defaults to var/locatedb within the installation. Create
that directory before running updatedb, or select another output/database
with updatedb --output and locate --database. The normal updatedb traversal
and user options still apply.

share/bootstrap/manifest.tsv records the installation layout and Buck source
labels. share/licenses contains the upstream licenses and launcher notices.
